; ============================================================================
; WORKSPACE DELETE -- AIF-078, steward ruling 2026-08-24 "A -- flag, never pack"
;
; THE VERB WAS RENAMED THE SAME DAY. It was WORKSPACE PURGE until the owner read
; its output: "how could you ever locate a purged row, it is gone forever,
; delete is a flag and that means the row still exists just ignored." The verb
; sets the delete flag and SUPERSEDED and the row stays on disk permanently --
; so the name promised removal and the code guaranteed the opposite. In xBase
; the pair is exact: DELETE flags, PACK removes. PURGE is still ACCEPTED as an
; alias and PG_T5 below is what keeps that true.
;
; This file and its spec id keep the name WSPURGE deliberately: a spec id is an
; allocated identifier with run history behind it, and renaming one to match a
; verb costs more than it buys.
; ============================================================================
;
; WHAT MAKES PG_T1 A DISCRIMINATOR AND NOT A DEMONSTRATION.
;
; WS_ID allocation is max(WS_ID) + 1, DERIVED by scanning the surviving catalog
; rows. Nothing persists a high-water mark. So if PURGE ever physically removed
; rows -- or if the allocator learned to skip delete-flagged ones -- purging the
; NEWEST rows would drop max back below them, and the next WORKSPACE NEW would
; re-mint an id a purged workspace already used.
;
; That is not cosmetic. D10.2 makes the chain-root WS_ID a workspace's DURABLE
; IDENTITY, so reissuing one gives two different workspaces the same identity at
; different times -- R5's defect on the time axis, where nothing can detect it
; after the fact.
;
; PG_T1 arranges exactly that trap: WSPRG1 is minted, retired, minted again and
; retired again, so its two rows are the two HIGHEST in the catalog. Both are
; purged. Then a NEW name is minted, and its id must be GREATER than the highest
; purged id. Pack, and this reads less-or-equal. One line, and it separates
; "flagged" from "gone".
;
; WHY PG_T2 AND PG_T3 ARE A PAIR.
;
; Ruling A has two halves and the row is WORSE OFF if only one lands. The delete
; flag hides the row from ordinary reads; SUPERSEDED=1 stops the catalog scan
; ELECTING it as live. scan_catalog does not filter deleted rows -- it elects on
; WS_NAME plus SUPERSEDED alone -- so a flag-only purge would leave a row that is
; invisible to the user and still adoptable by the next WORKSPACE NEW. Deleted
; and fine would look alike, which is the AIF-118 shape.
;
; PG_T2 reads the purged row WITH SET DELETED OFF and asserts SUPERSEDED = 1.
;
; PG_T3 WAS WRITTEN TO SHOW THE ROW GOES AWAY UNDER SET DELETED ON. IT DOES NOT.
; Measured on this spec's first run: LOCATE still reaches a delete-flagged row
; with deleted visibility HIDE (ON). So the sentence above about the flag hiding
; the row is TRUE OF THE INTENT AND FALSE OF THE ENGINE, and it is left standing
; with this correction beneath it rather than quietly rewritten -- the flag is
; what keeps the row COUNTED (preserving the high-water mark), and SUPERSEDED is
; what makes it unadoptable. See the arm itself for the full measurement.
;
; PG_T4 IS THE REFUSAL ARM, and it guards a rule that changed between design and
; code. The design said PURGE would refuse any name with a LIVE catalog row and
; send the caller to DESTROY first. Then DESTROY's dispatch was read: it resolves
; its target through the RUNTIME registry and answers "no such workspace" for
; anything not currently declared. A catalog-only live head -- the thirteen the
; 2026-08-24 census found -- is reachable by NEITHER verb under that rule. So
; what PURGE refuses is a workspace DECLARED IN THIS SESSION, which is what
; actually needs protecting.
;
; Every marker is a FIELD-VALUE comparison against WORKSPACES.dbf, per the
; doctrine FIELDMGR_APPEND paid for: a spec that asserts SHAPE passes green on a
; blanked table. Nothing here reads a console line.
;
; GUARDS: if any PG_G* reds, treat every PG_T* as UNPROVEN. The arms all depend
; on the two-row chain having actually been built, and on the ids being known.
;
; Leaves its own rows behind, flagged and superseded, BY DESIGN -- the file never
; shrinks and that is what ruling A chose. Cost is 4 rows per run.
; Explicit-run until soaked.
; ============================================================================

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
SET DELETED ON

; Prior-run residue. Refusals here are noise, not failure.
WORKSPACE DESTROY WSPRG1
WORKSPACE DESTROY WSPRG2
WORKSPACE DESTROY WSPRG3

; --- BUILD A TWO-ROW CHAIN AT THE TOP OF THE CATALOG ------------------------
; Mint, retire, mint again, retire again. WSPRG1 now owns the two highest
; WS_IDs in the catalog, which is what makes the pack hazard reachable.

WORKSPACE NEW WSPRG1
&& AIF-078 sec 4: '&_WORKSPACES', NOT 'workspaces'. DO NOT SIMPLIFY THIS BACK.
&& This spec WRITES through the WORKSPACES slot (WORKSPACE NEW) and READS
&& through the DBF slot (USE WORKSPACES). 'SET PATH DBF workspaces' is
&& DATA-relative and therefore PINNED TO PRODUCTION: bracket the WORKSPACES
&& slot for isolation and the mint lands in scratch while the LOCATE below
&& searches production, and every arm goes red for a reason unrelated to what
&& it tests. '&_WORKSPACES' is the engine-owned variable for that slot,
&& resolved from paths::state() at expansion time, so it FOLLOWS the bracket.
&& Proven 2026-08-28: with the slot moved to scratch, 'workspaces' still
&& resolved to data\workspaces while '&_WORKSPACES' resolved to the scratch
&& root. Identical when nothing is bracketed, which is why this is inert today.
SET PATH DBF &_WORKSPACES
USE WORKSPACES
LOCATE FOR ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "WSPRG1" .AND. ALLTRIM(SUPERSEDED) = "0"
VAR pid1 = WS_ID
? "PG_G0_first_row_located:" + (ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "WSPRG1")
CLOSE

WORKSPACE DESTROY WSPRG1
WORKSPACE NEW WSPRG1
SET PATH DBF &_WORKSPACES
USE WORKSPACES
LOCATE FOR ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "WSPRG1" .AND. ALLTRIM(SUPERSEDED) = "0"
VAR pid2 = WS_ID
? "PG_G1_second_row_is_newer:" + (WS_ID > $pid1)
CLOSE

WORKSPACE DESTROY WSPRG1

; Both rows are now superseded and nothing holds the name. Confirm the chain is
; really two rows before purging it -- an arm that purges one row and reports
; success proves less than it looks.
SET PATH DBF &_WORKSPACES
USE WORKSPACES
; NOT RECCOUNT(). USE_AGAIN measured that RECNO()/FOUND() render EMPTY in a '?'
; marker and that STR() does not rescue them; RECCOUNT() serves compile_predicate
; -- scan and FOR clauses over an open area -- not the marker path. A marker that
; errors PRINTS NOTHING rather than going red, so this guard would have vanished
; from the suite while the count still read full. Field comparison instead.
LOCATE FOR WS_ID = $pid1
? "PG_G2_first_row_still_present_before_purge:" + (ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "WSPRG1")
CLOSE

; --- THE PURGE --------------------------------------------------------------

WORKSPACE DELETE WSPRG1

; --- PG_T1: THE DISCRIMINATOR ----------------------------------------------
; A brand new name must mint ABOVE the highest purged id. If the purged rows
; had left the file, max would have fallen back below them and this reads .F.

WORKSPACE NEW WSPRG2
SET PATH DBF &_WORKSPACES
USE WORKSPACES
LOCATE FOR ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "WSPRG2" .AND. ALLTRIM(SUPERSEDED) = "0"
? "PG_G3_new_name_has_a_live_row:" + (ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "WSPRG2")
? "PG_T1_purge_did_not_lower_the_high_water_mark:" + (WS_ID > $pid2)
CLOSE

; --- PG_T2: THE ELECTION-BLOCKING HALF LANDED -------------------------------
; Read the purged row itself, with deleted rows visible.

SET DELETED OFF
SET PATH DBF &_WORKSPACES
USE WORKSPACES
LOCATE FOR WS_ID = $pid2
? "PG_G4_purged_row_still_on_disk:" + (ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "WSPRG1")
? "PG_T2_purged_row_is_superseded:" + (ALLTRIM(SUPERSEDED) = "1")
CLOSE

; --- PG_T3: SET DELETED ON DOES NOT HIDE THE ROW FROM LOCATE ----------------
;
; THIS ARM INVERTED ON ITS FIRST RUN, 2026-08-24, AND THE MEASUREMENT IS WORTH
; MORE THAN THE GREEN IT REPLACED.
;
; It was written to assert the opposite: park on a live row, LOCATE the purged
; one, and show the known occupant survived because the delete flag hid it.
; It read .F. -- LOCATE printed "Located." and moved the cursor onto the purged
; row. With SET DELETED ON, and the shell confirming "Deleted visibility: HIDE
; (ON)" one line earlier, a delete-flagged record is STILL REACHED by LOCATE.
;
; The flag itself landed. purge_durable_workspace re-reads isDeleted() from the
; record before counting a row done and refuses to continue if it is false, and
; the verb reported both rows purged -- so this is not a write that failed. It
; is LOCATE not consulting the setting.
;
; WHAT THAT CHANGES, and it is a correction to this spec's own header and to
; the purge design: the delete flag is NOT what protects the catalog. SUPERSEDED
; is. The flag's value is that scan_catalog still COUNTS the row, which is what
; preserves the high-water mark (PG_T1). Hiding was assumed, not measured, and
; the assumption was wrong.
;
; MEASURED FOR LOCATE ONLY. Whether LIST, SMARTLIST, COUNT or the FOR-scan path
; honour SET DELETED is UNMEASURED here and is not claimed either way.
;
; Both lines below are now a TRIPWIRE. If PG_T3 ever reads .F. again, LOCATE has
; GAINED delete-filtering, and the purge's safety argument gains a second layer
; that it does not have today -- repoint this arm deliberately rather than
; letting it retune itself. PG_T3B is the protection that actually holds, read
; off the same row in the same breath.

SET DELETED ON
SET PATH DBF &_WORKSPACES
USE WORKSPACES
LOCATE FOR ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "WSPRG2" .AND. ALLTRIM(SUPERSEDED) = "0"
? "PG_G5_parked_on_a_live_row:" + (ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "WSPRG2")
LOCATE FOR WS_ID = $pid2
; THE TRIPWIRE FIRED, 2026-08-24, AND THIS IS THE DELIBERATE REPOINT.
;
; PG_T3 recorded a measured defect: LOCATE reached a delete-flagged row with
; deleted visibility HIDE (ON). Its own instruction said that if it ever read
; .F. again, LOCATE had GAINED delete-filtering and the arm must be repointed
; on purpose rather than allowed to retune itself. It read .F. the same day.
;
; AIF-123 found why: filter::visible() -- the one gate LIST, COUNT, SMARTLIST,
; LOCATE, FIND, SCAN, EXPORT and logical_nav all ask -- applied SET FILTER and
; FOR and never consulted SET DELETED. The rung had been missing since
; 2025-08-16 (06ba79e93). It is back, and LOCATE now reports "Not Located."
;
; So the arm is inverted, and PG_T3B is repurposed rather than deleted. It used
; to read SUPERSEDED off the row LOCATE landed on; with no landing it was
; reading a stale cursor and would have gone red for a reason that had nothing
; to do with what it names. It now carries the OTHER HALF -- the same
; both-halves discipline SDVIS is built on, because "hidden under ON" means
; nothing without "visible under OFF" beside it.
? "PG_T3_locate_no_longer_reaches_a_purged_row_under_deleted_on:" + (ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "WSPRG2")
CLOSE

SET DELETED OFF
SET PATH DBF &_WORKSPACES
USE WORKSPACES
LOCATE FOR WS_ID = $pid2
? "PG_T3B_and_set_deleted_off_still_reaches_it:" + (ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "WSPRG1")
CLOSE

; --- PG_T4: THE REFUSAL ARM -------------------------------------------------
; A workspace declared in THIS session is refused, and survives the refusal
; with its live row intact.

WORKSPACE NEW WSPRG3
WORKSPACE DELETE WSPRG3
SET PATH DBF &_WORKSPACES
USE WORKSPACES
LOCATE FOR ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "WSPRG3" .AND. ALLTRIM(SUPERSEDED) = "0"
? "PG_T4_declared_workspace_kept_its_live_row:" + (ALLTRIM(SUPERSEDED) = "0")
CLOSE

; --- TEARDOWN, AND PG_T5 -----------------------------------------------------
; Retire then delete this spec's own names -- the DESTROY-then-DELETE flow the
; verb is designed around. The rows remain, flagged; the file never shrinks.

WORKSPACE DESTROY WSPRG2
WORKSPACE DESTROY WSPRG3
WORKSPACE DELETE WSPRG2

; --- PG_T5: THE PURGE ALIAS STILL DISPATCHES --------------------------------
;
; WSPRG3 is retired above and is deleted HERE, deliberately spelled the OLD way.
; -WithGui taught this lesson the same morning: a spelling kept for
; compatibility that nothing exercises is a spelling that rots, and the rot is
; only discovered by whoever's script breaks.
;
; THE ARM IS A FIELD-VALUE COMPARISON, not a run of the command. The first
; draft of this block was the WORKSPACE PURGE line and a comment calling itself
; PG_T5, with no marker behind it -- which asserts NOTHING. If PURGE stopped
; dispatching, the verb would print an error and the spec would still read all
; green. That is the FIELDMGR_APPEND defect committed inside the arm written to
; prevent it, caught before it ran.
;
; So: read WSPRG3's row back with deleted rows VISIBLE and assert SUPERSEDED=1.
; That is only true if the alias actually reached delete_durable_workspace.

WORKSPACE PURGE WSPRG3

SET DELETED OFF
SET PATH DBF &_WORKSPACES
USE WORKSPACES
LOCATE FOR ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "WSPRG3"
? "PG_T5_purge_alias_still_reaches_the_verb:" + (ALLTRIM(SUPERSEDED) = "1")
CLOSE

SET DELETED ON
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
